Subject: [PATCH] core: make analyze() switchable so its cost can be measured

analyze() walks all 61184 composited pixels through a 4096-slot open-addressed
set with linear probing, every frame, to fill two counters in the video report:
nonblack_pixels and unique_colors. The 3DS frontend reads neither.

The cost is unknown, which is the problem. T026 tried to speed up the frontend
render because it looked expensive, measured -1.1%, and that was noise. The
lesson there was to measure before optimising, and this makes the measurement
possible in the same session on the same build, by toggling rather than
rebuilding -- a rebuild between two arms would make them incomparable.

Default is 0, so the PC build and every existing caller are byte-for-byte
unchanged. This is a switch for the harness, not an optimisation.

When skipping, the counters are set to zero rather than left holding the
previous frame's values. A host that reads them while skipping must not be shown
stale numbers as if they were current.

Also documents a latent hazard that this makes reachable: the set has 4096
slots, but the reported distinct count is clamped at 1024, which implies the
author expected far more distinct values than the clamp admits. If distinct
ever exceeds 4096 the table is full, the probe loop finds neither an empty slot
nor a match, and `for (;;)` never terminates. It does not fire today because
the real distinct count is small. Not fixed here -- changing the probe would
alter report values, and that is not this patch's business.

--- a/internal/include/sc_core_internal.h
+++ b/internal/include/sc_core_internal.h
@@ -18,3 +18,23 @@
 uint32_t sc_crc32_bytes(const unsigned char *data, size_t size);
 
 #endif
+
+/* Diagnostic switch for analyze() in sc_v28_video.c.
+ *
+ * analyze() walks every composited pixel through a 4096-slot open-addressed
+ * set with linear probing, purely to fill two report counters. Hosts that do
+ * not read those counters can turn it off and stop paying for it.
+ *
+ * Default 0 -- unchanged behaviour, so the PC build and any existing caller
+ * get exactly what they got before. This exists so the cost can be MEASURED
+ * rather than assumed, which is the only reason it is switchable at all.
+ *
+ * Note the latent hazard this makes reachable to toggle: the set has 4096
+ * slots and the reported distinct count is clamped at 1024, implying the
+ * author expected more distinct values than that. If distinct ever exceeds
+ * 4096 the table is full, the probe loop finds neither an empty slot nor a
+ * match, and `for (;;)` never terminates. Do not raise expectations on
+ * SC_ANALYZE_SET_SLOTS without fixing that first. */
+extern int sc_core_skip_analyze;
+extern void sc_core_set_skip_analyze(int skip);
+extern int sc_core_get_skip_analyze(void);
--- a/src/simcity_static_recomp.c
+++ b/src/simcity_static_recomp.c
@@ -101,6 +101,10 @@
     return g_core_log != NULL;
 }
 
+int sc_core_skip_analyze = 0;
+void sc_core_set_skip_analyze(int skip) { sc_core_skip_analyze = skip ? 1 : 0; }
+int sc_core_get_skip_analyze(void) { return sc_core_skip_analyze; }
+
 void simcity_recomp_log_close(void) {
     if (!g_core_log) return;
     core_logf(NULL, "log-close", "");
--- a/src/sc_v28_video.c
+++ b/src/sc_v28_video.c
@@ -1137,8 +1137,15 @@
     result.reached_feature_set_supported =
         (uint8_t)(supported_lines == SC_V11_PPU_VISIBLE_LINES);
     if (full_frame) {
-        analyze(out32, output_pixels,
-                &result.nonblack_pixels, &result.unique_colors);
+        if (!sc_core_skip_analyze) {
+            analyze(out32, output_pixels,
+                    &result.nonblack_pixels, &result.unique_colors);
+        } else {
+            /* Zero rather than stale: a host reading these while skipping must
+               not see the previous frame's numbers presented as this frame's. */
+            result.nonblack_pixels = 0u;
+            result.unique_colors = 0u;
+        }
         if (sc_core_logging_enabled()) {
             sc_sha256_bytes((const unsigned char *)out555,
                             output_pixels * sizeof(*out555),
